			<div class="content" id="post-content-4849289">
									

<p>oh hey I got to this today woo.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>My central issue with this is that it feels too predictable: as soon as you realise something bad happens during the tour of duty that the notes are about, which is before it even starts, you can pretty much trace out the entire path of it. It feels&#8230;to me like the framing device sucks. Having all the notes be from one of the researchers means that you don't really get the kinda, personal, intimate reactions that you're looking for; but it also means that all the interesting bits are kinda&#8230;elided? It kinda ends up feeling that this spends far more time having Katherine be like &quot;wow this is so cool&quot; than it does actually being cool, y'know?</p>
<p>The pacing is kinda janky too: there's about 2500 words until stuff starts to go wrong, and it feels like it takes way too long for this to happen because like&#8230;until then, it feels very uh, generic? Particularly since e.g. we're told about the healing properties in the description so the bit where it's like 'wow it healed Amanda' is not hugely interesting or surprising. And then the whole descent into chaos is introduced, takes place and ends in the next 1000 words and it feels like it's just&#8230;kinda there rather than the core of the story, y'know? It doesn't help that there's 300 words wrapping it up, so it feels&#8230;weird.</p>
<p>I think this needs more characterisation: we get the feeling of Taylor being hesitant and it kinda works but everyone else just feels&#8230;generically there? I don't know what Katherine or Amanda like, or what Diego or Carmen like or anything; I don't really know what they're like as people; and because of the framing here, it feels too much like we're being told 'Taylor is scared' than shown Taylor acting in a way that conveys he's scared?</p>
<p>I wonder whether breaking the journal format occasionally would help: perhaps with Katherine attaching video logs of experiments so other characters can take the front stage a little at times and we can really see e.g. Amanda taking D-2 into the cavern for experimentation up front and personal and get like, a feeling for how they all feel about this?</p>
<p>It also kinda feels like this is too mysterious: there's a lot of suggestions that there's more going on than we are seeing, that deep in the SCP, something is happening; but there's nothing textual that sheds any light on this, and it feels unsatisfying as a result. I don't think we need to know exactly what's happening but just, a little bit of a hint at it would help? Something to really indicate that yeah, there definitely is something bigger happening and not just 'all these characters think there is for unclear reasons', y'know? I think the final part, where Katherine enters the submarine, would be great for this: you could really have her descend deeply into the SCP and find something but then turn into a crustacean before she has a chance to do anything with the knowledge?</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><em>Gonna note that I can miss stuff; flag things which are right as being wrong and indeed flag things which are deliberately wrong.</em><br />
<em>I'll likely use <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">strikethrough</span> to indicate stuff to remove and <span style="color: green">green text</span> to indicate stuff to add.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX’s location at the deepest point in the Marianas Trench (‘Challenger Deep’)is considered sufficient in containing it, as civilian interaction is unlikely, and expeditions to said location are easily tracked and monitored.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Something about this feels&#8230;iffy and I'm not quite sure what: |I think it's the vague nature of 'unlikely'. Part of me wonders whether cutting the explanation of why right down and just stating e.g. &quot;Physical containment measures around SCP-XXXX have been deemed unnecessary due to its position at Challenger Deep in the Marianas Trench' would work better?</p>
<p>Then again, the next paragraph indicates a provisional site has been constructed near it and I wonder whether just indicating that the cave is contained there and the site is like, around it would be better?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This site is to be staffed with research staff, two guards, and D-class as requested for testing, in order to continue researching SCP-XXXX. Provisional Site-86 is also to research better methods of containment, should civilian technology increase to the point where expeditions to SCP-XXXX’s location become more accessible, thus risking a containment breach scenario.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>These feel a touch unnecessary: my general advice is that ConProcs should cover either stuff that's absolutely necessary for containment or stuff that hints at things happening in the SCP; and this just sets up 'there is research happening' really. The sentence after is a little better in that it sets up the inaccessibility by the Foundation more.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Observing incident XXXX-1, SCP-XXXX anomalous effects are not to be used for human experimentation or in emergency situations</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Missing a full stop at the end here. 'Observing' also feels wrong: perhaps 'Following' would be better? Or 'Due to the events of Incident XXXX-1,&#8230;' (I'd also capitalise the i in 'incident' too)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX is the designation for a circular cave on the seafloor</p>
</blockquote>
<p>While you've used the ConProcs to establish where the SCP is, it feels like that should be repeated here? Like, this kinda, vague 'it's on the seafloor' statement feels overly vague?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It sits at a depth of 10,920 meters, and the entrance measures around 5 meters in diameter.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you specify the Marianas trench in the previous sentence, you can cut the depth here; and the entrance can be specified as e.g. &quot;for a 5m diameter circular cave&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The space <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">therein</span><span style="color: green">within SCP-XXXX</span> appears to be sphere-like in structure, measuring roughly 60 meters in diameter at its widest point.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'appears' feels&#8230;iffy here: generally, you want to say what stuff is, not what it looks like; 'approximately spherical' might be better than 'appears to be sphere-like' if you're wanting to convey 'not a perfect sphere'.</p>
<p>Same goes for 'roughly': it feels unclinical and vague.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Large tunnels seem to lead further down from SCP-XXXX’s core structure, their width varying,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'seem' implies there are not large tunnels here. I would advise using 'Large tunnels lead further&#8230;'; or even better, 'Large tunnels of varied with lead further down&#8230;' and then you can get rid of the &quot;,their width varying,&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>though averaging the same as XXXX’s entry point.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would cut this</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is unknown where these tunnels lead, as no manned expedition has been approved, and drones sent in have yielded an ‘out of range’ error after a certain distance.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence is fine but it feels&#8230;iffy because at this point, nothing has been established that would give us a clue as to why manned expeditions haven't been approved</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The current theory is that these tunnels lead somewhere extra-dimensional in nature, and not to the surface.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a bad sentence because it's telling us 'hey these tunnels lead somewhere strange' rather than showing that they lead somewhere strange by indicating what kinda strange place they go or why the foundation theorises this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Firstly: Hume readings in the area are incredibly low, indicating some force inside the cavern is altering reality.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd probably rephrase to 'The first is that&#8230;'; and the same for the 'Secondly:&#8230;' later</p>
<blockquote>
<p>(for reference please see Dr. Anna Lang’s research and SCP-3001)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This would work better if it was a footnote being used as references (e.g. if the research was given a journal paper esque title say)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>act wildly differently within the cavern</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'wildly differently' is both needlessly vague and extremely unclinical and I would probably use something from the follow up sentences in it's place.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>(see Document-XXXX-A for details)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd probably cut this or at least move it to a footnote; it interrupts the flow of the paragraph unnecessarily.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>(ie: a human or aquatic species)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;(i.e., a human or&#8230;)&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>these cells will become identical to the host, with little variation</p>
</blockquote>
<p>are they identical or is there variation?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Side effects have been reported however, and the investigation into this is considered a top priority for the project.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What side effects?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Addendum: Document XXXX-A</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I feel like a more descriptive title would help avoid needing a summary paragraph for it e.g. &quot;Addendum XXXX-A: Specimens from SCP-XXXX&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>request to Dr. Mcneil.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'Dr. McNeil'.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Investigation into local fauna interaction is ongoing</p>
</blockquote>
<p>missing period at the end of this; and it probably needs to be expanded a little too e.g. &quot;Investigation of their interactions with local fauna are ongoing&quot; say to make it clear what 'local fauna interaction' is referring to (i.e., what is interacting with local fauna should be more explicit)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>have been <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">spotted and</span> recovered clinging to the walls of SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'spotted' feels overly casual, 'observed' would be better but 'recovered' implies 'observed' too so it feels unnecessary to have both.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Specimens have also been spotted crawling into the tunnels leaving XXXX, though why is still unknown.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>See previous comment re. spotted; 'though why is still unknown' also feels awkward: I think the question needs to be explicit (e.g. &quot;though why they do this is currently unknown&quot;)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Multiple other small fish species are formed by SCP-XXXX though viewing and capturing them can be difficult</p>
</blockquote>
<p>something about the end of this feels&#8230;weirdly casual? It feels like something a kid would write in a school report and not like something you'd expect to see in a scientific paper.</p>
<p>I'd also include something to explicitly separate this from the previous section: perhaps a horizontal line (<span style="white-space: pre-wrap;">----</span> on a line between the two); since it kinda feels like it could be a second paragraph to the Helicoprion entry.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is also theorized that Helicorpion is the apex of a food chain ‘manufacture’ by SCP-XXXX, though why and where these specimens go is currently unknown</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'why and where these specimens go is currently unknown' feels super janky and honestly I don't really like any of this. I think you've gotta imply the food chain thing by commenting that e.g. Helicorpion specimens have been observed eating other specimens in the cave, and then just cut the whole thing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Addendum: Document XXXX-B</p>
</blockquote>
<p>same comment re. more descriptive addendum title to avoid needing lengthy paragraphs introducing the section. I would also not put the individual notes in their own collapsible: a shared collapsible could work or you could just, not have collapsibles.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Incident XXXX-1 refers to one of the first crews rotated into Provisional Site-86</p>
</blockquote>
<p>this feels janky as all hell, 'Incident' leads us to suspect a single thing e.g. 'Incident XXXX-1 was a containment breach that occurred on [date]'; whereas 'one of the first crews' feels&#8230;like an ongoing thing? This feels like perhaps it might need to be more specific about what exactly happened for it to work as a designation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>provided selections\of Researcher Kathrine Capullo's</p>
</blockquote>
<p>errant</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This is Dr. Katherine, marine biologist at Provisional Site-86, writing the first log for this six month shift.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I feel like the marine biologist and six month shift should be established in the paragraph before the note; introducing it like this feels just really odd.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We were transferred here via submersible, dispatched from a Foundation vessel.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I mean&#8230;this feels implied? I don't think any of it needs spelled out?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>until the mainland checks in <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">here</span>.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>We’ll be watching XXXX and logging stuff from it for about half a year</p>
</blockquote>
<p>it's a six month shift: the 'about half a year' feels needlessly uncertain; and 'stuff' is needlessly vague.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and then get to move on to hopefully more interesting SCP’s.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Given we're almost 1000 words in at this point, someone intimating the SCP isn't interesting is a bad sign; particularly since we haven't seen anything that indicates this is false yet!</p>
<blockquote>
<p>That’s about it in terms of what we’ve been left with in terms of standard gear, so I’d say that concludes this check in</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a super clunky sentence up to the comma, the second feels weirdly informal compared to the start and you're missing a period again.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Secondly, it’s ironic considering the fact this place looks like an 80’s sci fi horror. We’re only the second or third shift here and this site’s decently run down.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I feel like you want something more evocative than 'run down': really tell us about like, leaking pipes or something, y'know, things that are actually broken rather than a generic 'stuff'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We’ll put in a report for a look over after we’re done here.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think 'report' should be 'request'? or like 'I'll schedule a look over once we're done here'?</p>
<p>It's also unclear if 'done here' is 'done recording this message' or 'done with this six month stay'.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>That was mostly all the excitement today, accept for maybe Taylor.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'except maybe for Taylor'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This thing got of the worst spot and honestly I couldn’t have imagined it could have pulled something like this off.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'got of the worst spot' is&#8230;not something I can parse easily? I'm not sure what you're trying to say here.</p>
<p>That said: this is a junk sentence. Tell us about the cool dangerous thing happened, not vaguely that something cool and dangerous happened.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The findings are staggering, but I mean I have to start from the beginning.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>See earlier comment about junk sentence: all this conveys is 'something important happened' but you gotta show the something important first for us to care about it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Kind of needless to say by now</p>
</blockquote>
<p>not really, you've cut from 'we put amanda in the water' to 'amanda is fine' so we don't know this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>She’s got a mark where it healed up but <span style="color: green">she's</span> alive and walking</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>We’ll be running more test<span style="color: green">s</span> on these <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">kinds of</span> properties soon, but genuinely I can say I’m more excited about this thing now.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Unfortunately we were off to a rough start. D-1 scratched Carmen during a transfer in a violent outburst.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This feels&#8230;odd? Like it feels like there should be a lot better precautions in place to avoid this happening, y'know?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>with her escorting D-1 into the cavern</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>with approval we’re allowing Carmen to take D-1 out in the sub</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Do you mean D-2 in these, since D-2 is the one with the missing arm? There's a vague nod to 'heal the wound opened by Dr. Amanda' later too which correlated to the 'Amanda will open up a wound on his amputation'?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>any I’ve performed in former Foundation employment.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'any other I've performed in the Foundation' would be clearer.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>one of the most beneficial<span style="text-decoration: line-through;">s</span>.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>differential myself: Besides the massive difference in pressure</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'Besides' shouldn't start with a capital</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and so long as it continues<span style="color: green">,</span> we’ll be planning a tunnel expedition by February.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Samuels seems happy ‘his’ site is doing so well,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>why are there quotes around 'his'? This implies that they're like, not following Samuels orders or something.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Command skipped a check-in, said it was for a quarantine after that last discovery.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Are&#8230;are they doing check-in's via command physically sending someone down? Surely they'd be doing them via radio or w/e?</p>
<p>Also this is yet another place where you use words to indicate something interesting has happened rather than showing us something interesting happening.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Fifth is paralysis brought on by the mutation of tissue to things resembling crab shells, mollusk, coral, oysters, etc.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>you've skipped 'fourth'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The sixth step I’ll explain in detail: We had left Amanda in her quarters, as a modified morgue after step five</p>
</blockquote>
<p>this is a super janky sentence because everything up to the colon implies it's about to explain something in detail and then everything after the colon is not you explaining it in detail</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The D-class are a <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">hair breath for</span><span style="color: green">hair's breadth from</span> paralysis</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>when we heard the door break down. Amanda was totally covered, looked like a human coral reef, but we saw her crawling on all fours as fast as possible through the halls.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why did Amanda break the door down and then run away?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Hi.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would just cut this, you haven't started any other things like this.</p>
</div>
